import json,re
from pathlib import Path
p=Path(__file__).parent;r=json.loads((p/'reviews.json').read_text())
def add(s,v,t,tags,reason,round,number,statement=None,solution=None,page=1,**kw):
 id='che_2023_'+s;u='https://imosuisse.github.io/files/solutions/'+round+'-2023-solution.pdf';section={'final-round':'Final Round','selection':'IMO Selection','second-round':'Second Round'}[round]
 if not (p/(id+'.json')).exists():(p/(id+'.json')).write_text(json.dumps({'country':'Switzerland','competition':'Swiss Mathematical Olympiad','year':2023,'section':section,'problem_number':number,'problem_markdown':statement or '', 'solutions_markdown':[],'booklet_title':'Swiss '+section+' 2023'},indent=2))
 r[id]={'difficulty':v,'topic':t,'tags':tags,'reason':reason,'competition':'Swiss Mathematical Olympiad','section':section,'number':str(number),'sourceUrl':u,'solutionUrl':u+'#page='+str(page),'sourceName':'Swiss Mathematical Olympiad — official archive',**kw}
 if statement:r[id]['statement']=statement
 if solution:r[id]['solution']=solution
 return id
add('100eeb',3,'Geometry',['angle bisectors','cyclic quadrilaterals'],'Reflection and the arc-midpoint angle-bisector property give the required equal angles directly.','final-round',1)
add('e95add',4.5,'Combinatorics',['strategy','invariants'],'The opposite-direction reply has net displacement one; controlling the turn number proves that it remains safe.','final-round',2,solution=r'Brian wins. Use coordinates centred on the square, whose reachable safe lattice points have both coordinates in $[-n,n]$. Brian replies in the direction opposite to Albus. After $t$ complete pairs of moves the total displacement has length at most $t$, since each pair has net displacement one. For $t\le n$, Brian’s $t$th reply is therefore safe. If play reaches pair $n+1$, Albus must jump $2n+1$, which is impossible inside the square. Thus Brian never loses.',page=4)
add('131c4d',5.5,'Number theory',['recurrences','modular arithmetic','pigeonhole principle'],'View consecutive terms modulo a prime divisor as a finite-state cycle; its prime period forces a square-root bound.','final-round',3,page=5)
add('aaeace',5,'Algebra',['inequalities','substitution'],'Resolve signs, shift the variables by one, and combine a symmetric inequality with the product constraint.','final-round',4,page=6)
add('0dd769',5.5,'Algebra',['functional equations','restricted domain'],'The omitted codomain value forces the scalar in the second iterate to be zero or one; substitutions eliminate the exceptional case.','final-round',5,solution=r'Write $c=f(1)\ne-1$. Setting $x=y\ne0$ yields $f(f(x))=\alpha x$, where $\alpha=(1-c)/(1+c)$. If $\alpha\notin\{0,1\}$, the allowed input $x=-1/\alpha$ would give the forbidden value $-1$. If $\alpha=0$, then $c=1$ and $f(f(1))=f(1)=1$, a contradiction. Hence $\alpha=1$ and $c=0$. Thus $f(0)=f(f(1))=1$ and $f(f(0))=f(1)=0$, so $f(f(y))=y$ for every $y\in D$. Now $x=1$ gives $(1+y)f(y)+y=1$, whence $f(y)=(1-y)/(1+y)$. This maps $D$ to $D$, is an involution, and satisfies $(x+y)f(y/x)+y=x$ whenever the original equation is defined.',page=8)
add('final6',5.5,'Number theory',['largest prime','divisibility'],'The largest prime forces two preceding twin primes; reduction modulo three and a valuation check finish.','final-round',6,r'Find every integer $n\ge3$ for which $n!$ divides $\prod_{p<q\le n}(p+q)$, the product being over primes $p,q$.',page=9)
r['che_2023_final6'].update(solutionKind='source-link',solution='[Official worked solution](https://imosuisse.github.io/files/solutions/final-round-2023-solution.pdf#page=9). Check n=3,4,5,6 separately before the largest-prime argument.')
add('final7',5.5,'Geometry',['radical axis','reflection'],'Parallel lines locate the radical axis; reflection identifies a second useful point on it.','final-round',7,r'In acute $ABC$, let $F$ be the altitude foot from $A$, and $P\in AF$. Through $P$, parallels to $AC,AB$ meet $BC$ at $D,E$. On $(ABD),(ACE)$ choose $X,Y\ne A$ with $DX=DA$, $EY=EA$. Prove $B,C,X,Y$ are concyclic.',page=10)
r['che_2023_final7'].update(solutionKind='source-link',solution='[Official worked solution](https://imosuisse.github.io/files/solutions/final-round-2023-solution.pdf#page=10).')
add('7618c8',6,'Combinatorics',['invariants','construction','induction'],'An odd-divisor obstruction and a constructive reduction of arbitrary power-of-two totals are both needed.','final-round',8,solution=r'''The answer is one pile when $n$ is a power of two, and two otherwise.

First, any configuration with total $2^k$ can be consolidated. Pair up its odd piles and remove one pebble from each member of each pair, creating piles of size two. All piles are now even; divide their sizes by two and apply induction. Implementing the resulting moves at twice their sizes completes the consolidation.

Write $n=2^k+r$, $0<r<2^k$. We construct a pile of size $r$, after which the remaining total $2^k$ can be consolidated by the lemma. Put $h=2^{k-1}$. If $r=h$, simply form piles of sizes $2^k,r$. If $r<h$, form two piles of size $h$ and remove $h-r$ from both, leaving two piles of size $r$; reserve one. If $r>h$, form piles of sizes $2^k,h$ and remove $2^k-r$ from both, leaving the larger pile of size $r$; reserve it. In both cases there are enough initial pebbles and the removal amount is positive and does not exceed the smaller pile.

Finally let $c>1$ be the odd part of $n$. Initially not all pile sizes are divisible by $c$. This remains true: if after a move every size were divisible by $c$, the new pile $2x$ would give $c\mid x$, so both original sizes would also have been divisible by $c$. A single pile of size $n$ would contradict this invariant.''',page=12)
add('b388e6',5.5,'Combinatorics',['extremal construction','counting'],'Take all bushes of the majority colour, then prove a matching bound using alternating monochromatic pairs.','selection',1,solution=r'''The guaranteed value is $507$. Suppose red is the majority colour, with $R\ge1012$ red bushes and $B\le1011$ blue bushes. Visit every red bush, skipping alternate bushes in each intervening blue run. At most $\lfloor B/2\rfloor$ blue roses are required, so $r-b\ge507$.

For sharpness use a blue singleton followed by 1011 alternating pairs $RR,BB,RR,BB,\ldots,RR$. If the walk meets $k$ pairs, each met pair contributes at most two roses and each interior pair at least one. More directly, after removing any unfavourable endpoint bushes, a red-favouring walk begins and ends in red pairs: for $m$ red pairs it meets at least $m-1$ blue pairs, so its score is at most $2m-(m-1)=m+1\le507$. A blue-favouring walk confined to the paired part meets at most 505 blue pairs and scores at most 506. If it also uses the initial singleton, between it and each later blue pair is a red pair, giving at most $1+2m-m\le506$. Thus no walk exceeds 507.''',page=2)
