add('d46f87',6,'Combinatorics',['injective maps','extremal sets'],'Raise weights on one maximizing subset; positivity makes the maximum unique and the map invertible.','selection',12,solution=r'For each $g:[n]\to[n]$, choose a set $A\in\mathcal S$ maximizing $\sum_{a\in A}g(a)$ (break ties by any fixed ordering). Define $f(i)=g(i)+1$ for $i\in A$, and $f(i)=g(i)$ otherwise. For $B\ne A$, the difference of its new weight from that of $A$ is governed by $$\sum_A f-\sum_B f=\sum_A g-\sum_B g+|A\setminus B|.$$ This is positive unless $A\subset B$; in that case positivity of $g$ already contradicts the maximizing choice of $A$. Thus $f$ has unique maximizing set $A$. Recover $A$ from $f$, then subtract one on $A$ to recover $g$. This is an injection from the $n^n$ maps $g$ into the required functions.',page=33)
