add('c8c186',5.5,'Combinatorics',['tilings','induction'],'Prove the four longest strips must occupy the corners, giving exactly two orientations per concentric layer.','second-round','C2',solution=r'''There are $2^n$ tilings. First all rectangle edges have integer coordinates: a horizontal or vertical line avoiding the finitely many edges partitions the side length into integer lengths, so every tile edge it crosses has integer coordinate.

A vertical strip of length $2n$ therefore touches the top or bottom. If it touches neither vertical side, the widths available on either side are less than $2n$, so both horizontal strips of length $2n$ would have to lie in the remaining single row. This is impossible. Thus each of the four longest strips contains a corner. They contain different corners, and the nonoverlap condition forces either the clockwise or the counterclockwise arrangement around the border. Removing them leaves a $(2n-1)\times(2n-1)$ square with precisely the analogous smaller tiles. There are two choices at each of the $n$ stages, and each choice works.''')
add('b4de88',2.5,'Geometry',['angle chasing','cyclic quadrilaterals'],'The angle trisectors and angle bisector produce one cyclic quadrilateral, after which the parallelism is immediate.','second-round','G1')
add('61eeae',4,'Geometry',['tangent circles','cyclic quadrilaterals'],'Two isosceles triangles place F on the auxiliary circle; the diameter and tangent then give four right angles.','second-round','G2',r'Let $JK$ be a diameter of circle $\omega_1$ and $U\ne J$ lie on its tangent at $J$. The smaller circle centred at $U$ tangent to $\omega_1$ touches it at $Y$. Let $I\ne J$ be the second intersection of $JK$ with $(JYU)$, and $F\ne Y$ the second intersection of $KY$ with the circle centred at $U$. Prove that $FUJI$ is a rectangle.',solution=r'Let $O$ be the centre of $\omega_1$. Tangency gives collinear $U,Y,O$. Since $UF=UY$ and $OY=OK$, angle chasing gives $\angle YFU=\angle UYF=\angle OYK=\angle YKJ=\angle YJU$. Thus $J,Y,U,F$ are concyclic, and $F$ lies on $(JYU)$. Now $UJ\perp JI$ because $UJ$ is tangent at $J$, while $JY\perp YF$ because $JK$ is a diameter. The corresponding angles of the circle $(JYU)$ show that all four angles of $FUJI$ are right angles.')
